    "lem-nrqmx-magnetic-form-closability": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-dependent-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ be a binary relation on $X$.\nCall $R$ **entire on $X$** when\n\n$$\\text{for every } x \\in X \\text{ there is } y \\in X \\text{ with } x \\mathbin{R} y .$$\n\nThe **Axiom of Dependent Choice**, written $\\mathrm{DC}$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every nonempty set $X$, every relation $R$ entire on $X$, and every\n> $a \\in X$, there is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to X$ (A function is a relation $f$ with $(a,b) \\in f$ and $(a,c) \\in f$ implying $b = c$; $f : A \\to B$, the value $f(a)$, domain and codomain,\n> The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann)) with\n> $$x_0 = a \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1} \\ \\text{ for every } n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$$\n\nHere a sequence in $X$ means a function from $\\mathbb{N}$ to $X$, not necessarily\na real-valued sequence. As everywhere in this library $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, and the sequence is\nindexed from $0$; the term $x_0$ is the prescribed starting point $a$ and every\nlater term is related to its predecessor.\n\n**What DC adds to what came before.** Choice function and\nThe Axiom of Choice select one element from each member of a family that is\nfixed in advance, and The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) does the same for a family indexed\nby $\\mathbb{N}$. In both, the family is given before any selection is made. DC is\nthe principle needed when the $n$-th set to select from is not known until the\nfirst $n$ selections have been made: here the admissible values of $x_{n+1}$ are\nexactly the $R$-successors of $x_n$, so the family being chosen from is built\nalong the choosing. Thus the displayed recursion is directly an instance of\nDC; it is not itself an application of the fixed-family formulation of\ncountable choice. This observation concerns the input to the two axioms and\nmakes no claim about a nonimplication between them.\n\n**The starting point may be dropped.** The formally weaker statement obtained by\ndeleting the clause $x_0 = a$ — for every nonempty $X$ and every entire $R$ there\nis a sequence with $x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for all $n$ — is an immediate\nconsequence of the form above, since $X$ is nonempty and any of its elements may\nbe taken as $a$. Conversely, assume the start-free form, and fix $X$, $R$ and $a$ as above. Let $P$ be the set of all functions $p:k\\to X$ with nonzero $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $p(0)=a$, and $p(i)\\mathbin R p(i+1)$ whenever $i+1<k$. It is a set by Separation inside $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N\\times X)$ (The power set $\\mathcal{P}(x) = \\{\\, z : z \\subseteq x \\,\\}$, The Cartesian product $A \\times B := \\{\\, z \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(A \\cup B)) : \\exists a \\in A\\ \\exists b \\in B\\ z = (a,b) \\,\\}$, The Axiom Schema of Separation: for each formula $\\varphi$, $\\forall \\bar p\\,\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,\\forall z\\,(z \\in y \\leftrightarrow (z \\in x \\wedge \\varphi(z,\\bar p)))$); a natural is the set of its predecessors (On $\\mathbb{N}$ the order is membership: $m < n \\iff m \\in n$). The singleton path $\\{(0,a)\\}$ lies in $P$.\n\nOn $P$ let $p\\mathbin S q$ mean that $q$ extends $p$ by exactly one new last entry. This relation is entire: for one particular $p:k\\to X$, write $k=j+1$ (Every nonzero natural number is a successor); an $R$-successor $y$ of $p(j)$ exists, and $q=p\\cup\\{(k,y)\\}$ is the required extension. This is existential instantiation for that path, not a simultaneous choice of successors. Start-free DC now supplies a sequence $(p_n)$ with $p_n\\mathbin S p_{n+1}$; its first path need not be the singleton path, but every path in $P$ already starts at $a$.\n\nTheir graphs are nested. By induction (The principle of mathematical induction), $p_n$ has at least $n+1$ entries: the initial path is nonempty, and every step adds one entry. Hence $x=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb N}p_n$ is a function with domain $\\mathbb N$ and values in $X$. Indeed, nestedness makes all common values agree, every natural index eventually lies in a path, and no path has indices outside $\\mathbb N$. Each path starts at $a$, so $x(0)=a$; for any $i$, a sufficiently long path contains both $i$ and $i+1$, giving $x(i)\\mathbin R x(i+1)$. Replacement and Union form this union of the sequence of graphs. Thus the start-free and prescribed-start forms are equivalent in ZF. Every use below retains the prescribed-start convention.\n\n**$R$ need not be an order and the terms need not be distinct.** What DC delivers\nis a sequence, that is a function $\\mathbb{N} \\to X$, not a chain in the\norder-theoretic sense (Chain in a poset). The relation may be symmetric, and the\nsequence may repeat a value or be constant; all that is asserted is\n$x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ at every index.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-magnetic-form-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. Work in\n$H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}u\\overline v$, linear in the first\nvariable, using the quotient and complex-function conventions of the linked\nEuclidean $L^2$ definitions. Fix real scalars $m>0$, $\\hbar>0$, and $q$.\nLet $A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_d)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R^d)$ be real and\nlet $V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy\n$V\\ge-v_0$ almost everywhere for some finite $v_0\\ge0$. Put\n$\\mathscr D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $u\\in\\mathscr D$,\ndefine\n$$\\Pi_j^A u=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_j u-qA_j u\\qquad (1\\le j\\le d).$$\nFor $u,v\\in\\mathscr D$ define the sesquilinear form\n$$q_{A,V}(u,v)=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\langle\\Pi_j^A u,\\Pi_j^A v\\rangle+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V(x)u(x)\\overline{v(x)}\\,dx,$$\nand write $q_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}(u,u)$. Each integral is finite: the test\nfunctions and their magnetic derivatives are bounded with compact support,\nand $V$ is integrable on compact sets. The form is Hermitian, densely\ndefined, and bounded below by $-v_0\\|u\\|_2^2$.\n\nFor $\\varepsilon_0>0$ define its shifted form norm by\n$$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=q_{A,V}[u]+(v_0+\\varepsilon_0)\\|u\\|_2^2.$$\nThe form is **closable** when every sequence $(u_n)\\subset\\mathscr D$ with\n$\\|u_n\\|_2\\to0$ and\n$\\|u_n-u_m\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$ as $n,m\\to\\infty$ also satisfies\n$\\|u_n\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$. This condition is independent of the\nchoice of $\\varepsilon_0>0$. When it holds, the **closed form closure** is the\ncompletion of $\\mathscr D$ in this norm, identified with its $L^2$ limits;\nthe corresponding closed form is denoted $\\overline q_{A,V}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-position-and-momentum-operators",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Fix\n$\\hbar>0$ and an integer $d\\ge1$, and work in the complex Hilbert space\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with inner product\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f\\overline g$ linear in the first\nargument ([[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]],\n[[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]],\n[[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]).\n\n**Weak derivatives.** For $f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ and\n$j\\in\\{1,\\dots,d\\}$, say that the $j$-th weak derivative of $f$ exists in\n$L^2$ when there is a class $g\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f\\,D_j\\varphi=(-1)\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}g\\,\\varphi \\qquad\\text{for every }\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d),$$\nwhere $D_j\\varphi$ is the classical partial derivative and the pairing is\nbilinear without conjugation ([[def-weak-derivative-of-a-locally-integrable-function]]).\nSuch a $g$ is unique as an $L^2$ class\n([[lem-weak-derivatives-are-unique-almost-everywhere]]); write $D_jf:=g$.\nThe Sobolev space $W^{1,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ is the set of classes\n$f\\in L^2$ for which $D_1f,\\dots,D_df$ all exist in $L^2$, and\n$W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ is the set of $f\\in W^{1,2}$ with\n$D_jf\\in W^{1,2}$ for every $j$, with $D_jD_kf$ written for the iterated\nderivative ([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]).\n\n**(a) Position operators.** For $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,d\\}$ define\n$$D(Q_j):=\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C):\\ x\\mapsto x_jf(x)\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)\\},\\qquad (Q_jf)(x):=x_jf(x).$$\nThus $Q_jf$ is the class of the pointwise product with the $j$-th coordinate\nfunction.\n\n**(b) Momentum operators.** For $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,d\\}$ define\n$$D(P_j):=\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C):\\ D_jf\\text{ exists in }L^2\\},\\qquad P_jf:=-i\\hbar\\,D_jf .$$\n\nBoth $Q_j$ and $P_j$ are densely defined $\\mathbb C$-linear operators in\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$: for $Q_j$ the compactly supported smooth\nclasses lie in the domain because $x_j$ is bounded there, and for $P_j$ they\nlie in the domain because their classical derivatives are weak derivatives in\n$L^2$ ([[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]).\n\nFor density, the classes of $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ are dense in\n$W^{0,2}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ by\n[[cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn]] and\n[[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]. Since these classes lie in each domain\nas above, both operator domains are dense.\n\nFor a class\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d)$ one has $Q_jf=x_jf$ and\n$P_jf=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_jf$ pointwise. The self-adjointness of these operators\nand their Fourier representation are proved in\nthe companion self-adjointness lemma on this page, not\nasserted here; for $d=1$ the domain $D(P)$ of the single momentum operator\ncoincides with $W^{1,2}(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g\\in\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$, then\n\n$$\\int |fg|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|g\\|_2.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $f,g$ is zero almost everywhere,\nor there is a constant $c>0$ with\n\n$$|f|^2=c\\,|g|^2\\qquad\\mu\\text{-almost everywhere}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axioms of Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. For $F,G\\in AC[a,b]$,\n$$\\int_a^bFG'+\\int_a^bF'G=F(b)G(b)-F(a)G(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-magnetic-form-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. Work in\n$H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}u\\overline v$, linear in the first\nvariable, using the quotient and complex-function conventions of the linked\nEuclidean $L^2$ definitions. Fix real scalars $m>0$, $\\hbar>0$, and $q$.\nLet $A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_d)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R^d)$ be real and\nlet $V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy\n$V\\ge-v_0$ almost everywhere for some finite $v_0\\ge0$. Put\n$\\mathscr D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $u\\in\\mathscr D$,\ndefine\n$$\\Pi_j^A u=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_j u-qA_j u\\qquad (1\\le j\\le d).$$\nFor $u,v\\in\\mathscr D$ define the sesquilinear form\n$$q_{A,V}(u,v)=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\langle\\Pi_j^A u,\\Pi_j^A v\\rangle+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V(x)u(x)\\overline{v(x)}\\,dx,$$\nand write $q_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}(u,u)$. Each integral is finite: the test\nfunctions and their magnetic derivatives are bounded with compact support,\nand $V$ is integrable on compact sets. The form is Hermitian, densely\ndefined, and bounded below by $-v_0\\|u\\|_2^2$.\n\nFor $\\varepsilon_0>0$ define its shifted form norm by\n$$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=q_{A,V}[u]+(v_0+\\varepsilon_0)\\|u\\|_2^2.$$\nThe form is **closable** when every sequence $(u_n)\\subset\\mathscr D$ with\n$\\|u_n\\|_2\\to0$ and\n$\\|u_n-u_m\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$ as $n,m\\to\\infty$ also satisfies\n$\\|u_n\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$. This condition is independent of the\nchoice of $\\varepsilon_0>0$. When it holds, the **closed form closure** is the\ncompletion of $\\mathscr D$ in this norm, identified with its $L^2$ limits;\nthe corresponding closed form is denoted $\\overline q_{A,V}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\varepsilon_0>0$ and put $W=V+v_0$. For every $u\\in C_c^\\infty$,\n$$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\|\\Pi_j^A u\\|_2^2+\\|W^{1/2}u\\|_2^2+\\varepsilon_0\\|u\\|_2^2.$$\nThis identity shows that if $(u_n)$ is Cauchy in the shifted form norm,\nthen each sequence $(\\Pi_j^A u_n)$ and $(W^{1/2}u_n)$ is Cauchy in $L^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Suppose additionally that $\\|u_n\\|_2\\to0$. Let $w_j$ be the $L^2$\nlimit of $\\Pi_j^A u_n$. For any $\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty$, symmetry [F2]\nand Cauchy–Schwarz give\n$$\\langle w_j,\\phi\\rangle=\\lim_n\\langle\\Pi_j^A u_n,\\phi\\rangle=\\lim_n\\langle u_n,\\Pi_j^A\\phi\\rangle=0.$$\nDensity in [F1] implies $w_j=0$. If $w_0$ is the $L^2$ limit of\n$W^{1/2}u_n$, then [F3] and Cauchy–Schwarz similarly give\n$$\\langle w_0,\\phi\\rangle=\\lim_n\\langle u_n,W^{1/2}\\phi\\rangle=0$$\nfor every test function $\\phi$, so density gives $w_0=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Every term on the right side of the norm identity in step 1.1 therefore\ntends to zero, including $\\varepsilon_0\\|u_n\\|_2^2$. Thus\n$\\|u_n\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$, which is exactly the closability\ncriterion in [[def-nrqmx-magnetic-form-data]]. The completion is densely\nembedded in $L^2$ because it contains the dense core, and the criterion makes\nthat embedding injective; its form is closed by construction and retains the\nlower bound $-v_0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
