    "lem-nrqmx-landau-form-diagonalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-magnetic-form-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. Work in\n$H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}u\\overline v$, linear in the first\nvariable, using the quotient and complex-function conventions of the linked\nEuclidean $L^2$ definitions. Fix real scalars $m>0$, $\\hbar>0$, and $q$.\nLet $A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_d)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R^d)$ be real and\nlet $V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy\n$V\\ge-v_0$ almost everywhere for some finite $v_0\\ge0$. Put\n$\\mathscr D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $u\\in\\mathscr D$,\ndefine\n$$\\Pi_j^A u=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_j u-qA_j u\\qquad (1\\le j\\le d).$$\nFor $u,v\\in\\mathscr D$ define the sesquilinear form\n$$q_{A,V}(u,v)=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\langle\\Pi_j^A u,\\Pi_j^A v\\rangle+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V(x)u(x)\\overline{v(x)}\\,dx,$$\nand write $q_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}(u,u)$. Each integral is finite: the test\nfunctions and their magnetic derivatives are bounded with compact support,\nand $V$ is integrable on compact sets. The form is Hermitian, densely\ndefined, and bounded below by $-v_0\\|u\\|_2^2$.\n\nFor $\\varepsilon_0>0$ define its shifted form norm by\n$$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=q_{A,V}[u]+(v_0+\\varepsilon_0)\\|u\\|_2^2.$$\nThe form is **closable** when every sequence $(u_n)\\subset\\mathscr D$ with\n$\\|u_n\\|_2\\to0$ and\n$\\|u_n-u_m\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$ as $n,m\\to\\infty$ also satisfies\n$\\|u_n\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$. This condition is independent of the\nchoice of $\\varepsilon_0>0$. When it holds, the **closed form closure** is the\ncompletion of $\\mathscr D$ in this norm, identified with its $L^2$ limits;\nthe corresponding closed form is denoted $\\overline q_{A,V}$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-magnetic-form-closability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. Let $d,m,\\hbar,A,V,v_0$ and\n$q_{A,V}$ be the magnetic form on the test core, with $m,\\hbar>0$,\n$A\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R^d)$, $V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$\nreal-valued and $V\\ge-v_0$ almost everywhere. Then $q_{A,V}$ is closable on\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ in the shifted form norm\n$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=q_{A,V}[u]+(v_0+\\varepsilon_0)\\|u\\|_2^2$\nfor every $\\varepsilon_0>0$. Its closure is a densely defined closed\nHermitian form bounded below by $-v_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, with the componentwise integral and quotient conventions of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]], [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]] and [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]. Define\n$$\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\mathcal Ff(\\xi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(x)\\exp(-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi)\\,dx,\\qquad \\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n.$$\nHere $x\\cdot\\xi=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\\xi_j$, Lebesgue measure is used, and the exponential is [[def-complex-exponential]]. Its unit modulus follows from [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]. The integral is evaluated using any measurable representative. Absolute convergence and representative independence are the obligations discharged by [[lem-l-one-fourier-transform-is-well-defined]]. The output is a function defined at every frequency, not merely an almost-everywhere class. At frequency zero the formula reads $\\widehat f(0)=\\int f$. No choice selection of representatives for a family of classes is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-plancherel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and let $n\\ge1$. Fourier transformation on Schwartz space extends uniquely to a surjective complex-linear isometry $\\mathcal F_2:L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$. It preserves the first-variable-linear inner product, and hence is unitary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. The Fourier transform is a topological automorphism of $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$, with $\\mathcal F^2=R$ and $\\mathcal F^{-1}=R\\mathcal F$, where $Rf(x)=f(-x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]), let $a,b\\ge1$, and let $\\lambda_n$ denote Lebesgue measure on $\\mathcal L(\\mathbb R^n)$ as defined in [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]] and completed from Borel measure as in [[cor-lebesgue-sigma-algebra-is-the-completion-of-borel-lebesgue-measure]]. The product measure $\\lambda_a\\times\\lambda_b$ exists by [[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]], and under $\\mathbb R^{a+b}=\\mathbb R^a\\times\\mathbb R^b$, $\\lambda_{a+b}$ is its completion ([[thm-euclidean-lebesgue-measure-is-the-completion-of-the-product-of-lebesgue-measures]]). Write $H_a=L^2(\\mathbb R^a,\\lambda_a)$ and $H_b=L^2(\\mathbb R^b,\\lambda_b)$, with $L^2$ understood as the almost-everywhere quotient of square-integrable measurable functions ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]). Then there is a unique linear isometry\n\n$$\\Theta:H_a\\widehat\\otimes H_b\\longrightarrow L^2(\\mathbb R^{a+b})$$\n\nwith $\\Theta(f\\otimes g)(x,y)=f(x)g(y)$ for all $f\\in H_a$, $g\\in H_b$ and a.e. $(x,y)$, and $\\Theta$ is onto: it is a surjective linear isometry, hence unitary. Vector-valued or spin factors follow by finite component sums. No identification of non-Euclidean product measures is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axioms of Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. For $F,G\\in AC[a,b]$,\n$$\\int_a^bFG'+\\int_a^bF'G=F(b)G(b)-F(a)G(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Fix real parameters $m>0$, $\\omega>0$, $\\hbar>0$, put $\\ell:=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(m\\omega)}>0$, and let $g_0,P,(g_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be the Hermite data of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence]] in $L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$, so that $(g_n)$ is a complete orthonormal family by [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-completeness]]. Define\n\n$$D:L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R,dx),\\qquad (Df)(x):=\\ell^{-1/2}f(x/\\ell),$$\n\nand let $A$ be the self-adjoint diagonal operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma]] for the complete orthonormal family $(g_n)$ and the eigenvalues $\\lambda_n=2n+1$, with the core $P$ and spectrum $\\{2n+1:n\\in\\mathbb N\\}$ ([[thm-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-spectrum]]).\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $D$ is a linear isometry of $L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$ onto $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx)$ with two-sided inverse $(D^{-1}g)(y)=\\ell^{1/2}g(\\ell y)$; the functions $\\psi_n:=Dg_n$, $\\psi_n(x)=\\ell^{-1/2}g_n(x/\\ell)$, form a complete orthonormal family of $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx)$;\n\n(b) the operator\n\n$$H_{\\mathrm{osc}}:=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\,DAD^{-1},\\qquad D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}):=\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R,dx):D^{-1}f\\in D(A)\\},$$\n\nis self-adjoint, satisfies $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}\\psi_n=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)\\psi_n$ for every $n$, and has\n\n$$D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})=\\Bigl\\{f:\\sum_n\\hbar^2\\omega^2(n+\\tfrac12)^2|c_n|^2<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=\\sum_n\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)c_n\\psi_n,$$\n\nwhere $c_n:=\\langle D^{-1}f,g_n\\rangle=\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle$; its spectrum is $\\sigma(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})=\\{\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12):n\\in\\mathbb N\\}$, its ground energy is $\\hbar\\omega/2$ with eigenvector $\\psi_0$, and $\\langle H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f,f\\rangle\\ge\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\|f\\|^2$ for every $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$;\n\n(c) $D(P)=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\psi_n\\}$ is a core for $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, and for every polynomial $p$ the function $f:=D(pg_0)$ is $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R$ with\n\n$$H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}f''+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2f$$\n\npointwise on $\\mathbb R$;\n\n(d) for every $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and every test function $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$,\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f)(x)\\varphi(x)\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(x)\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\varphi''(x)+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2\\varphi(x)\\Bigr)dx,$$\n\nso $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ is the self-adjoint realization of the differential expression $-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2$ in the weak sense; no identification of $D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ with a separately defined maximal differential-operator domain is asserted.\n\nAll parameters enter as positive real numbers; no physical identification of $m,\\omega,\\hbar$ with measured quantities is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. If $f : V \\to \\mathbb C$ belongs to $L^1(\\lambda_n)$,\nthen\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axioms of Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. For $F,G\\in AC[a,b]$,\n$$\\int_a^bFG'+\\int_a^bF'G=F(b)G(b)-F(a)G(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-the-standard-smooth-step-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\beta$ be the standard flat function. The **standard smooth step function** is\n$$ \\sigma(t):=\\frac{\\beta(t)}{\\beta(t)+\\beta(1-t)}. $$\nBecause $\\beta$ is smooth and positive on $(0,\\infty)$, the denominator is positive on $(0,1)$, while $\\sigma(t)=0$ for $t\\le 0$ and $\\sigma(t)=1$ for $t\\ge 1$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, $k\\in\\mathbb N_0$, $1\\le p<\\infty$ and\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. Then the compactly supported smooth\nfunctions $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ are dense in\n$W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$: for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ with\n$$\\|\\varphi-u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n)}<\\delta.$$\nNo such norm-density assertion is made for $p=\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$L^2(\\mu)$ be the quotient of $\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$ by the almost-everywhere zero\nfunctions ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]), with the quotient\nnorm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[thm-the-l-p-norm-descends-to-the-quotient-and-makes-l-p-a-normed-space]]).\n\n1. On **real** $L^2(\\mu)$ the formula\n   $$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int fg\\,d\\mu$$is a well-defined inner product, linear in both variables, positive definite, with $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu)$ is a real Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). 2. On **complex** $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ the formula$$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int f\\overline g\\,d\\mu$$\n   is a well-defined inner product, linear in the first variable and\n   conjugate-linear in the second, positive definite, with\n   $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ is a complex\n   Hilbert space ([[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]).\n\nIn both cases the inner product induces exactly the established quotient\n$L^2$ norm.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On the dense tensor core $a(x)b(y)$ with $a,b\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R)$,\n$\\mathcal F_y$ is the identity on the first factor and the unitary Fourier\ntransform on the second, by [F2] and the Euclidean $L^2$ product identification\nin [F2]. It extends uniquely to a unitary on $L^2(\\mathbb R^2)$. For each\n$k$, translation $x=\\xi+x_0(k)$ preserves the $x$-integral by [F4]; Fubini\nthen shows that $Wg(\\xi,k)=g(\\xi+x_0(k),k)$ is an isometry with inverse\n$W^{-1}h(x,k)=h(x-x_0(k),k)$. Hence $U=W\\mathcal F_y$ is unitary.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$ and write\n$F(\\xi,k)=Uu(\\xi,k)$. For each fixed $k$, $F(\\cdot,k)$ is smooth and\ncompactly supported in $\\xi$. Plancherel in $y$, the derivative identity\nin [F2], and the substitution $x=\\xi+x_0(k)$ give\n$$q_B[u]=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}|\\partial_\\xi F|^2+\\frac{(qB)^2}{2m}\\xi^2|F|^2\\right)d\\xi\\,dk.$$\nFor a compactly supported smooth $g(\\xi)$, the oscillator differential\nexpression in [F3] belongs to $L^2$, so $g\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$.\nIntegration by parts against each $\\psi_n$ gives\n$\\langle H_{\\mathrm{osc}}g,\\psi_n\\rangle=E_n\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle$.\nCompleteness and Parseval yield\n$$\\langle H_{\\mathrm{osc}}g,g\\rangle=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty E_n|\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2.$$\nIntegration by parts also identifies the left side with\n$$\\frac1{2m}\\left(\\hbar^2\\|g'\\|_2^2+(qB)^2\\|\\xi g\\|_2^2\\right).$$\nApply this identity to $F(\\cdot,k)$ for almost every $k$. Tonelli [F6]\npermits integrating the nonnegative series, so the displayed core form is\nthe coefficient form on every transformed core vector.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $N<\\infty$ and $f_0,\\ldots,f_N\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R_k)$,\n$F_N(\\xi,k)=\\sum_{n=0}^Nf_n(k)\\psi_n(\\xi)$ has inverse\n$$U^{-1}F_N(x,y)=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\sum_{n=0}^N\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{iky}f_n(k)\\psi_n\\!\\left(x-\\frac{\\hbar k}{qB}\\right)dk.$$\nThe integrands have compact $k$-support and are Schwartz in $x$ uniformly\nthere by the Hermite form in [F3]; differentiating under the integral and\nintegrating by parts in $k$ show rapid decay in $y$, so the inverse is\nSchwartz. Define\n$$\\chi_R(x,y)=\\prod_{s\\in\\{x,y\\}}\\sigma(2-s/R)\\sigma(2+s/R),$$\nusing [F5]. These cutoffs are one on $[-R,R]^2$, vanish outside\n$[-2R,2R]^2$, and have $|\\nabla\\chi_R|\\le C/R$. For each covariant\nderivative,\n$$\\Pi_j^A(\\chi_Rv)=\\chi_R\\Pi_j^Av-i\\hbar(\\partial_j\\chi_R)v.$$\nDominated convergence sends the $L^2$ tails of $v$ and $\\Pi_j^Av$ to zero,\nand the extra term has norm at most $C\\hbar\\|v\\|_2/R$. Thus each $F_N$\nlies in the transformed form closure. Finite coefficient families are dense\nin the weighted target: truncate its convergent nonnegative series, then\napproximate the remaining finitely many $L^2(k)$ coefficients by\n$C_c^\\infty(k)$ using [F7].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Step 2.1 maps the transformed core isometrically into the weighted\ntarget. By [F8] the target is complete, so the isometric image of the form\ncompletion is closed there. Step 3.1 places a dense subspace of that target\nin the image; hence the image is the whole weighted space and the form\nclosure is exactly the stated one.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F8",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem assumes qB is nonzero."
