    "thm-nrqmx-periodic-flux-spectrum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-dependent-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ be a binary relation on $X$.\nCall $R$ **entire on $X$** when\n\n$$\\text{for every } x \\in X \\text{ there is } y \\in X \\text{ with } x \\mathbin{R} y .$$\n\nThe **Axiom of Dependent Choice**, written $\\mathrm{DC}$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every nonempty set $X$, every relation $R$ entire on $X$, and every\n> $a \\in X$, there is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to X$ (A function is a relation $f$ with $(a,b) \\in f$ and $(a,c) \\in f$ implying $b = c$; $f : A \\to B$, the value $f(a)$, domain and codomain,\n> The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann)) with\n> $$x_0 = a \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1} \\ \\text{ for every } n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$$\n\nHere a sequence in $X$ means a function from $\\mathbb{N}$ to $X$, not necessarily\na real-valued sequence. As everywhere in this library $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, and the sequence is\nindexed from $0$; the term $x_0$ is the prescribed starting point $a$ and every\nlater term is related to its predecessor.\n\n**What DC adds to what came before.** Choice function and\nThe Axiom of Choice select one element from each member of a family that is\nfixed in advance, and The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$) does the same for a family indexed\nby $\\mathbb{N}$. In both, the family is given before any selection is made. DC is\nthe principle needed when the $n$-th set to select from is not known until the\nfirst $n$ selections have been made: here the admissible values of $x_{n+1}$ are\nexactly the $R$-successors of $x_n$, so the family being chosen from is built\nalong the choosing. Thus the displayed recursion is directly an instance of\nDC; it is not itself an application of the fixed-family formulation of\ncountable choice. This observation concerns the input to the two axioms and\nmakes no claim about a nonimplication between them.\n\n**The starting point may be dropped.** The formally weaker statement obtained by\ndeleting the clause $x_0 = a$ — for every nonempty $X$ and every entire $R$ there\nis a sequence with $x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for all $n$ — is an immediate\nconsequence of the form above, since $X$ is nonempty and any of its elements may\nbe taken as $a$. Conversely, assume the start-free form, and fix $X$, $R$ and $a$ as above. Let $P$ be the set of all functions $p:k\\to X$ with nonzero $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $p(0)=a$, and $p(i)\\mathbin R p(i+1)$ whenever $i+1<k$. It is a set by Separation inside $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N\\times X)$ (The power set $\\mathcal{P}(x) = \\{\\, z : z \\subseteq x \\,\\}$, The Cartesian product $A \\times B := \\{\\, z \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathcal{P}(A \\cup B)) : \\exists a \\in A\\ \\exists b \\in B\\ z = (a,b) \\,\\}$, The Axiom Schema of Separation: for each formula $\\varphi$, $\\forall \\bar p\\,\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,\\forall z\\,(z \\in y \\leftrightarrow (z \\in x \\wedge \\varphi(z,\\bar p)))$); a natural is the set of its predecessors (On $\\mathbb{N}$ the order is membership: $m < n \\iff m \\in n$). The singleton path $\\{(0,a)\\}$ lies in $P$.\n\nOn $P$ let $p\\mathbin S q$ mean that $q$ extends $p$ by exactly one new last entry. This relation is entire: for one particular $p:k\\to X$, write $k=j+1$ (Every nonzero natural number is a successor); an $R$-successor $y$ of $p(j)$ exists, and $q=p\\cup\\{(k,y)\\}$ is the required extension. This is existential instantiation for that path, not a simultaneous choice of successors. Start-free DC now supplies a sequence $(p_n)$ with $p_n\\mathbin S p_{n+1}$; its first path need not be the singleton path, but every path in $P$ already starts at $a$.\n\nTheir graphs are nested. By induction (The principle of mathematical induction), $p_n$ has at least $n+1$ entries: the initial path is nonempty, and every step adds one entry. Hence $x=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb N}p_n$ is a function with domain $\\mathbb N$ and values in $X$. Indeed, nestedness makes all common values agree, every natural index eventually lies in a path, and no path has indices outside $\\mathbb N$. Each path starts at $a$, so $x(0)=a$; for any $i$, a sufficiently long path contains both $i$ and $i+1$, giving $x(i)\\mathbin R x(i+1)$. Replacement and Union form this union of the sequence of graphs. Thus the start-free and prescribed-start forms are equivalent in ZF. Every use below retains the prescribed-start convention.\n\n**$R$ need not be an order and the terms need not be distinct.** What DC delivers\nis a sequence, that is a function $\\mathbb{N} \\to X$, not a chain in the\norder-theoretic sense (Chain in a poset). The relation may be symmetric, and the\nsequence may repeat a value or be constant; all that is asserted is\n$x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ at every index.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-periodic-ring-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let\n$S^1=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ with angular coordinate $\\phi$, and identify\n$L^2(S^1;\\mathbb C)$ with $L^2((0,2\\pi),d\\phi;\\mathbb C)$; the omitted\nendpoint has measure zero. Let $H^2(0,2\\pi)$ be the interval Sobolev space whose elements and first two weak derivatives are in $L^2$. For $f\\in H^2(0,2\\pi)$ use the continuous endpoint representatives of $f$ and $f'$, and denote their traces by $f^*(0),f^*(2\\pi)$ and $(f')^*(0),(f')^*(2\\pi)$. Define\nthe periodic second-order domain\n$$H^2_{\\mathrm{per}}(S^1)=\\{f\\in H^2(0,2\\pi):f^*(0)=f^*(2\\pi),\\ (f')^*(0)=(f')^*(2\\pi)\\}.$$\nIt is the domain used for the periodic realization of the second derivative;\nit imposes no boundary at the angular seam beyond periodic matching.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-interval-unitary-boundaries",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, Countable Choice, and Dependent Choice. Let\n$0<L<\\infty$, let $H=L^2(0,L;\\mathbb C)$, and let $T_{\\min}$ and\n$T_{\\max}=T_{\\min}^*$ be the minimal and maximal realizations of\n$-D^2$ from the preceding interval-domain lemma. Fix a positive reference\nlength $\\ell$. For $f\\in H^2(0,L)$ define\n$\\Gamma_0f=(f(0),f(L))$ and\n$\\Gamma_1f=(f'(0),-f'(L))$. Let\n$U(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=UU^*=I_2\\}$, where $U^*$ is conjugate\ntranspose. For each $U\\in U(2)$ define\n$$D(T_U)=\\{f\\in H^2(0,L):(U-I_2)\\Gamma_0f+i\\ell(U+I_2)\\Gamma_1f=0\\},\\qquad T_Uf=-D^2f.$$\nThen $T_{\\min}\\subseteq T_U\\subseteq T_{\\max}$, each $T_U$ is self-adjoint,\nand every self-adjoint realization between $T_{\\min}$ and $T_{\\max}$ equals\nexactly one $T_U$. The reference length makes the value and scaled derivative\nboundary vectors have the same units if the interval coordinate is assigned\nunits; in the dimensionless mathematical model they are numerical vectors.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-sphere-onb",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\sigma$ be the polar surface measure on\n$S^2$ ([[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]) and let $(Y_{lm})_{l\\ge0,\\ -l\\le m\\le l}$ be the\nfunctions of [[thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-associated-polynomial-normalization]] ([[def-complex-exponential]]).\nThen $(Y_{lm})$ is a complete orthonormal family of the complex Hilbert space $L^2(S^2,\\sigma)$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]], [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]):\nthe family is orthonormal, and if $F\\in L^2(S^2,\\sigma)$ satisfies\n$\\int_{S^2}F\\overline{Y_{lm}}\\,d\\sigma=0$ for all $l\\ge0$ and $-l\\le m\\le l$, then $F=0$ almost everywhere.\nConsequently every $F$ has the expansion\n$$F=\\sum_{l=0}^\\infty\\sum_{m=-l}^{l}\\langle F,Y_{lm}\\rangle Y_{lm},\\qquad \\|F\\|^2=\\sum_{l,m}|\\langle F,Y_{lm}\\rangle|^2,$$\nwith $\\langle F,Y_{lm}\\rangle=\\int_{S^2}F\\overline{Y_{lm}}\\,d\\sigma$ and finite-subset-net convergence\n([[thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-kato-rellich",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Work on a complex Hilbert space with inner product linear in the first variable. Let $A$ be self-adjoint and let $B$ be symmetric with $D(A)\\subseteq D(B)$ and\n$A$-bound less than one ([[def-relative-boundedness-with-respect-to-an-operator]]).\nThen $A+B$ with domain $D(A)$ is self-adjoint. If $A$ is merely essentially\nself-adjoint and $B$ is symmetric with $D(A)\\subseteq D(B)$ and $A$-bound less\nthan one, then $A+B$ on $D(A)$ is essentially self-adjoint and its closure is\nthe self-adjoint operator obtained by applying the first part to $\\overline A$\nand the graph-norm extension of $B|_{D(A)}$ to $D(\\overline A)$. If $A\\ge\\gamma$ and\n$(a,b)$ is an admissible pair for $B$ with $a<1$, then\n$A+B\\ge\\gamma-\\max\\{a|\\gamma|+b,\\ b/(1-a)\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-relative-boundedness-with-respect-to-an-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be a linear operator on $H$ with domain $D(A)$. An operator $B$ is\n**$A$-bounded**, or **relatively bounded with respect to $A$**, when\n$D(A)\\subseteq D(B)$ and there are finite constants $a,b\\ge0$ with\n$$\\|Bx\\|\\le a\\|Ax\\|+b\\|x\\|\\qquad\\text{for all }x\\in D(A).$$\nThe infimum of the admissible constants $a$ is the **$A$-bound** of $B$, and\none says the $A$-bound is *below one* when some $a<1$ is admissible.\n\n**Equivalent form.** $B$ is $A$-bounded exactly when $B$ is bounded on $D(A)$\nfor the graph norm $\\|x\\|_A=(\\|x\\|^2+\\|Ax\\|^2)^{1/2}$ of\n[[def-unbounded-linear-operator-domain-and-graph]]: each estimate\n$\\|Bx\\|\\le a\\|Ax\\|+b\\|x\\|$ gives\n$\\|Bx\\|\\le\\max(a,b)\\sqrt2\\,\\|x\\|_A$, and conversely a graph-norm bound\n$\\|Bx\\|\\le C\\|x\\|_A$ gives the estimate with $a=b=C$. The constant $b$ is not\nintrinsic, and no closedness, density or resolvent hypothesis is needed for\nthe definition. If $A$ is closed with nonempty resolvent set, then $A$-bounded\noperators are exactly those with $D(A)\\subseteq D(B)$ for which $BR_A(z)$ is\nbounded for some, equivalently every, $z\\in\\rho(A)$\n([[def-resolvent-and-spectrum-of-a-closed-unbounded-operator]]). Indeed, an\n$A$-bound makes $BR_A(z)$ bounded because\n$AR_A(z)=zR_A(z)-I$; conversely, if $BR_A(z)$ is bounded, then\n$Bx=BR_A(z)(z-A)x$ gives an $A$-bound. Thus boundedness for one resolvent\nimplies relative boundedness and hence boundedness for every resolvent. This\nis used in the Kato-Rellich theorem below and recorded here as an interface.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T$ be a self-adjoint operator on the nonzero\ncomplex Hilbert space $H$. Then there is a unique regular projection valued\nmeasure $E$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ such that\n$$\nD(T)=\\Bigl\\{x\\in H:\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda^2\\,dE_x(\\lambda)<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad Tx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,dE(\\lambda)x\\quad(x\\in D(T)).\n$$\nConversely, if $E$ is a regular projection valued measure on $\\mathbb R$, then\nthe operator $\\int\\lambda\\,dE$ with that domain is self-adjoint and its\nspectral projection valued measure is $E$ again.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-intervals-integration-by-parts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axioms of Choice, Countable Choice and Dependent Choice\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]], [[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]).\nUse the spaces of [[def-nrqm-intervals-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]] and the\nabsolutely continuous representatives supplied by\n[[lem-nrqm-intervals-weak-derivative-ac-representative]].\n\n(a) *Bounded intervals.* Let $0<L<\\infty$ and $u,v\\in H^1(0,L)$. Then\n$\\int_0^Lu\\,\\overline{v'}=u^*(L)\\overline{v^*(L)}-u^*(0)\\overline{v^*(0)}\n-\\int_0^Lu'\\,\\overline v .$\n\n(b) *Endpoint-vanishing space.* $H^1_0(0,L)=\\{u\\in H^1(0,L):u^*(0)=u^*(L)=0\\}$,\nwith the traces $u^*(0),u^*(L)$ of the continuous representative.\n\n(c) *Second-order identity.* For $u,v\\in H^2(0,L)$,\n$\\int_0^L\\bigl(u''\\overline v-u\\,\\overline{v''}\\bigr)\n=\\bigl[u'^*\\overline{v^*}-u^*\\overline{v'^*}\\bigr]_0^L,$\nwhere the bracket denotes\n$u'^*(L)\\overline{v^*(L)}-u^*(L)\\overline{v'^*(L)}\n-u'^*(0)\\overline{v^*(0)}+u^*(0)\\overline{v'^*(0)}$.\n\n(d) *Half-line and line.* If $u,v\\in H^1(0,\\infty)$, then\n$\\int_0^\\infty u\\overline{v'}=-u^*(0)\\overline{v^*(0)}-\\int_0^\\infty u'\\overline v$, both integrals\nbeing absolutely convergent. For $u,v\\in H^1(\\mathbb R)$ one instead has\n$\\int_{\\mathbb R}u\\overline{v'}=-\\int_{\\mathbb R}u'\\overline v$,\nwith both integrals absolutely convergent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Set $A_0=-\\kappa\\partial_\\phi^2$ on $H^2_{\\mathrm{per}}(S^1)$. In the\nboundary coordinates $\\Gamma_0f=(f(0),f(2\\pi))$ and\n$\\Gamma_1f=(f'(0),-f'(2\\pi))$, the unitary swap matrix in [F1] makes the\nboundary equation equivalent to $f(0)=f(2\\pi)$ and\n$f'(0)=f'(2\\pi)$: adding and subtracting its two scalar equations gives\nthese two equalities. Thus $A_0$ is self-adjoint. Let $P=-i\\partial_\\phi$.\nIntegration by parts [F5] and equality of the endpoint traces show that $P$ is\nsymmetric on $H^2_{\\mathrm{per}}$. For $f=\\sum_nc_ne_n$ in this domain,\nintegration by parts gives $\\langle Pf,e_n\\rangle=nc_n$ and\n$\\langle A_0f,e_n\\rangle=\\kappa n^2c_n$. Parseval for [F2] therefore\nimplies $\\|Pf\\|_2^2=\\sum_n n^2|c_n|^2$ and\n$\\|A_0f\\|_2^2=\\kappa^2\\sum_n n^4|c_n|^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On $D(A_0)$ write\n$T_\\alpha=A_0+B_\\alpha$ with\n$B_\\alpha=-2\\kappa\\alpha P+\\kappa\\alpha^2I$. This operator is symmetric\nbecause $P$ is symmetric and $\\alpha,\\kappa$ are real. For every\n$\\varepsilon>0$ and integer $n$,\n$|n|\\le\\varepsilon n^2+(4\\varepsilon)^{-1}$; the coefficient identities\nin step 1.1 and the triangle inequality give\n$\\|B_\\alpha f\\|_2\\le a\\|A_0f\\|_2+b\\|f\\|_2$ with any prescribed $a>0$\nafter choosing $\\varepsilon$ sufficiently small and then a finite $b$.\nApplying [F3] with $a<1$ proves that $T_\\alpha$ is self-adjoint on the\nstated periodic domain.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Each $e_n$ lies in the periodic domain and direct differentiation gives\n$T_\\alpha e_n=\\kappa(n-\\alpha)^2e_n$. Completeness [F2] makes this the full\northonormal eigenbasis. The associated spectral projections are therefore\nthe sums of the character projections whose eigenvalues lie in the chosen\nBorel set, so every scalar spectral measure is supported on\n$\\{\\kappa(n-\\alpha)^2:n\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$. This set has no finite accumulation\npoint because $\\kappa>0$ and $|n-\\alpha|\\to\\infty$. If $z$ is outside it,\n$\\inf_n|\\lambda_n-z|>0$ and the diagonal inverse with coefficients\n$(\\lambda_n-z)^{-1}$ is bounded and maps onto $D(T_\\alpha)$; if $z=\\lambda_n$\nthen $e_n$ is a nonzero kernel vector. Hence the spectrum is exactly the\ndisplayed set and there is no continuous spectral measure.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $j\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $U_jf=e^{ij\\phi}f$. It is unitary, preserves\nthe periodic $H^2$ domain, and\n$(P-\\alpha-j)U_jf=U_j(P-\\alpha)f$ by differentiation; squaring gives\n$T_{\\alpha+j}U_j=U_jT_\\alpha$. If a smooth periodic unit-modulus function\n$u$ satisfies the displayed conjugacy, then $(P-\\alpha)u=0$, or\n$u'=i\\alpha u$.\nThe solutions are $u(\\phi)=c e^{i\\alpha\\phi}$ with $|c|=1$, which are\nperiodic exactly when $e^{2\\pi i\\alpha}=1$, equivalently $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb Z$.\nConversely, for integral $\\alpha$ this multiplier is single-valued and\nperiodic.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
