    "pthm-nrqmx-landau-direct-integral": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-nrqmx-magnetic-model-extension",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Use SI units with mass $m>0$ in kilograms, charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in\ncoulombs, action constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, vector potential\n$A$ in tesla-metres, magnetic field $B=\\nabla\\times A$ in teslas, and\nscalar potential-energy function $V$ in joules. For $d\\ge1$ and smooth real\n$A:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, with real\n$V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d)$ bounded below, adopt the minimal\ncoupling assignment whose core form is\n$$q_{A,V}[u]=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\|(-i\\hbar\\partial_j-qA_j)u\\|_2^2+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V|u|^2,\\qquad u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C).$$\nUse its closed form closure and the unique self-adjoint representing\nHamiltonian. For dimensional form norms use the fixed reference energy\n$E_0=1\\,\\mathrm J$: apply the closed-form representation to\n$q_{A,V}/E_0$ and multiply the representing operator by $E_0$.\n\nFor an optional spin-half degree of freedom in three dimensions, define\n$$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad S_j=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}\\sigma_j.$$\nFor a core spinor $u=(u_1,u_2)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^2)$,\ndefine the spin-independent form\n$$q^{(2)}_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}[u_1]+q_{A,V}[u_2].$$\nFor a declared dimensionless real coupling $g$, adopt the effective Pauli core\nform\n$$q^{\\mathrm P}_{A,V,g}[u]=q^{(2)}_{A,V}[u]-\\frac{gq}{2m}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(x)^*(S\\cdot B(x))u(x)\\,dx,$$\nwhere $u^*$ is conjugate transpose and $(S\\cdot B)(x)=\\sum_jS_jB_j(x)$.\nInclude this term only in model instances where the resulting form is\nclosable and bounded below, and use its closed form closure and representing\nHamiltonian. No universal semiboundedness for arbitrary $A,V,g$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-landau-form-diagonalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, $m,\\hbar>0$, and real $q,B$ with $qB\\ne0$. On\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ close the nonnegative form\n$$q_B[u]=\\frac1{2m}\\left(\\|-i\\hbar\\partial_xu\\|_2^2+\\|(-i\\hbar\\partial_y-qBx)u\\|_2^2\\right),\\qquad u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C).$$\nPut $\\omega_c=|qB|/m$,\n$E_n=\\hbar\\omega_c(n+\\tfrac12)$, and let $(\\psi_n)_{n\\ge0}$ be the\ncomplete normalized eigenfunctions of the one-dimensional oscillator with\nmass $m$, frequency $\\omega_c$, and action constant $\\hbar$ in its exact\nself-adjoint oscillator realization. For\nSchwartz $u$ define\n$\\mathcal F_yu(x,k)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-iky}u(x,y)\\,dy$,\nand use its unitary $L^2$ extension. Put $x_0(k)=\\hbar k/(qB)$ and\n$(Uu)(\\xi,k)=\\mathcal F_yu(\\xi+x_0(k),k)$.\nThen $U$ is unitary from $L^2(\\mathbb R^2_{x,y})$ onto\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^2_{\\xi,k})$. Writing\n$$Uu(\\xi,k)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty f_n(k)\\psi_n(\\xi),\\qquad f_n\\in L^2(\\mathbb R_k),$$\nthe form closure domain and value are exactly\n$$D(\\overline q_B)=\\left\\{u:\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty E_n\\|f_n\\|_{L^2(k)}^2<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad \\overline q_B[u]=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty E_n\\|f_n\\|_{L^2(k)}^2.$$\nThus the form is the diagonal oscillator form with an unrestricted $L^2(k)$\ndegeneracy factor; it asserts no finite-area state count.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-closed-form-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space, let $Q\\subseteq H$ be a dense linear subspace, and let\n$q:Q\\times Q\\to\\mathbb C$ be a symmetric sesquilinear form, linear in its\nfirst variable. Write $q[u]=q(u,u)$. Suppose that for some $c\\ge0$,\n$q[u]\\ge-c\\|u\\|^2$ for every $u\\in Q$, and that $Q$ is complete in the form\nnorm $\\|u\\|_{q,c}^2=q[u]+(c+1)\\|u\\|^2$. Then there is a unique self-adjoint\noperator $H_q\\ge-cI$ such that\n$$D((H_q+cI)^{1/2})=Q,\\qquad q(u,v)=\\langle H_qu,v\\rangle\\quad(u\\in D(H_q),\\ v\\in Q).$$\nEquivalently, $D(H_q)$ consists exactly of those $u\\in Q$ for which there is\n$f\\in H$ satisfying $q(u,v)=\\langle f,v\\rangle$ for all $v\\in Q$, and then\n$H_qu=f$. Under the $e^{tA}$ generator convention for associated forms, the\nassociated operator is $A=-H_q$. If $q_0$ is a densely defined closed symmetric\nform with $q_0[u]\\ge0$ and $v$ is a symmetric sesquilinear form on $Q(q_0)$\nsatisfying\n$$|v[u,u]|\\le a q_0[u]+b\\|u\\|^2\\quad(u\\in Q(q_0)),\\qquad 0\\le a<1,\\ b\\ge0,$$\nthen $q_0+v$ is closed and bounded below by $-b$ on the same domain. It has a\nunique self-adjoint representing operator there. The form bound is not an\noperator-relative bound.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Fix real parameters $m>0$, $\\omega>0$, $\\hbar>0$, put $\\ell:=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(m\\omega)}>0$, and let $g_0,P,(g_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ be the Hermite data of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence]] in $L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$, so that $(g_n)$ is a complete orthonormal family by [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-completeness]]. Define\n\n$$D:L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R,dx),\\qquad (Df)(x):=\\ell^{-1/2}f(x/\\ell),$$\n\nand let $A$ be the self-adjoint diagonal operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma]] for the complete orthonormal family $(g_n)$ and the eigenvalues $\\lambda_n=2n+1$, with the core $P$ and spectrum $\\{2n+1:n\\in\\mathbb N\\}$ ([[thm-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-spectrum]]).\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $D$ is a linear isometry of $L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$ onto $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx)$ with two-sided inverse $(D^{-1}g)(y)=\\ell^{1/2}g(\\ell y)$; the functions $\\psi_n:=Dg_n$, $\\psi_n(x)=\\ell^{-1/2}g_n(x/\\ell)$, form a complete orthonormal family of $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx)$;\n\n(b) the operator\n\n$$H_{\\mathrm{osc}}:=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\,DAD^{-1},\\qquad D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}):=\\{f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R,dx):D^{-1}f\\in D(A)\\},$$\n\nis self-adjoint, satisfies $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}\\psi_n=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)\\psi_n$ for every $n$, and has\n\n$$D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})=\\Bigl\\{f:\\sum_n\\hbar^2\\omega^2(n+\\tfrac12)^2|c_n|^2<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=\\sum_n\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)c_n\\psi_n,$$\n\nwhere $c_n:=\\langle D^{-1}f,g_n\\rangle=\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle$; its spectrum is $\\sigma(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})=\\{\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12):n\\in\\mathbb N\\}$, its ground energy is $\\hbar\\omega/2$ with eigenvector $\\psi_0$, and $\\langle H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f,f\\rangle\\ge\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\|f\\|^2$ for every $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$;\n\n(c) $D(P)=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\psi_n\\}$ is a core for $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, and for every polynomial $p$ the function $f:=D(pg_0)$ is $C^\\infty$ on $\\mathbb R$ with\n\n$$H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}f''+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2f$$\n\npointwise on $\\mathbb R$;\n\n(d) for every $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and every test function $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$,\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f)(x)\\varphi(x)\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(x)\\Bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\varphi''(x)+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2\\varphi(x)\\Bigr)dx,$$\n\nso $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ is the self-adjoint realization of the differential expression $-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2$ in the weak sense; no identification of $D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ with a separately defined maximal differential-operator domain is asserted.\n\nAll parameters enter as positive real numbers; no physical identification of $m,\\omega,\\hbar$ with measured quantities is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T$ be a self-adjoint operator on the nonzero\ncomplex Hilbert space $H$. Then there is a unique regular projection valued\nmeasure $E$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ such that\n$$\nD(T)=\\Bigl\\{x\\in H:\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda^2\\,dE_x(\\lambda)<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad Tx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,dE(\\lambda)x\\quad(x\\in D(T)).\n$$\nConversely, if $E$ is a regular projection valued measure on $\\mathbb R$, then\nthe operator $\\int\\lambda\\,dE$ with that domain is self-adjoint and its\nspectral projection valued measure is $E$ again.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The hypotheses $m,\\hbar>0$ and $qB\\ne0$ make\n$\\omega_c=|qB|/m>0$ and the translation\n$x_0(k)=\\hbar k/(qB)$ well-defined. Apply [F1]: the unitary $U$ sends the\nclosed form to the coefficient form\n$$\\mathfrak q[F]=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty E_n\\|f_n\\|_{L^2(k)}^2,$$\nwhere $F(\\xi,k)=\\sum_nf_n(k)\\psi_n(\\xi)$ and $(\\psi_n)$ is the complete\noscillator basis from [F3]. In particular,\n$\\mathfrak q[F]\\ge E_0\\|F\\|_2^2>0$ for nonzero $F$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply the closed-form representation theorem [F2] to $\\mathfrak q$. If\n$F=(f_n)$ is in the representing operator domain, there is $G=(g_n)$ in the\ncoefficient Hilbert space such that $\\mathfrak q(F,V)=\\langle G,V\\rangle$\nfor every form-domain vector $V$. Fix $n$ and take $V$ to have sole nonzero\ncomponent $V_n=E_nf_n-g_n$. It lies in the form domain because $f_n,g_n\\in\nL^2(k)$ and $E_n$ is finite. The representation identity gives\n$$0=\\mathfrak q(F,V)-\\langle G,V\\rangle=\\|E_nf_n-g_n\\|_2^2,$$\nso $g_n=E_nf_n$ for every $n$. Since $G$ belongs to the Hilbert space,\n$\\sum_nE_n^2\\|f_n\\|_2^2<\\infty$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Conversely, suppose $F$ is in the form domain and\n$\\sum_nE_n^2\\|f_n\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Define $G_n=E_nf_n$, which is a vector in\nthe coefficient Hilbert space. For every form-domain vector $V=(v_n)$,\nCauchy–Schwarz gives absolute convergence and\n$$\\mathfrak q(F,V)=\\sum_n E_n\\langle f_n,v_n\\rangle=\\sum_n\\langle G_n,v_n\\rangle=\\langle G,V\\rangle.$$\nThus [F2] represents the operator by $(f_n)\\mapsto(E_nf_n)$ on exactly\nthe stated domain. Its uniqueness clause identifies this operator with the\npostulated Hamiltonian.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For each $n$, the eigenspace at $E_n$ is\n$L^2(\\mathbb R_k)\\otimes\\mathbb C\\psi_n$, which is infinite-dimensional;\nthe energies are strictly increasing and tend to $+\\infty$. If\n$z\\notin\\{E_n:n\\ge0\\}$, then $\\inf_n|E_n-z|>0$ and\n$\\sup_n E_n/|E_n-z|<\\infty$. The diagonal inverse is therefore bounded and\nmaps into the operator domain, so $z$ is in the resolvent. Each $E_n$ is an\neigenvalue, and the complete coefficient decomposition together with [F4]\nshows that all spectral projections are sums of eigenspace projections.\nHence every spectral measure is atomic on the displayed set and the spectrum\nis exactly that set.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
