          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "case": "half flux",
          "calculation": "alpha=1/2 gives n=0 and n=1 at energy kappa/4",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 2.1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "pthm-nrqmx-pauli-square-identity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-nrqmx-magnetic-model-extension",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Use SI units with mass $m>0$ in kilograms, charge $q\\in\\mathbb R$ in\ncoulombs, action constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, vector potential\n$A$ in tesla-metres, magnetic field $B=\\nabla\\times A$ in teslas, and\nscalar potential-energy function $V$ in joules. For $d\\ge1$ and smooth real\n$A:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, with real\n$V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d)$ bounded below, adopt the minimal\ncoupling assignment whose core form is\n$$q_{A,V}[u]=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\|(-i\\hbar\\partial_j-qA_j)u\\|_2^2+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V|u|^2,\\qquad u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C).$$\nUse its closed form closure and the unique self-adjoint representing\nHamiltonian. For dimensional form norms use the fixed reference energy\n$E_0=1\\,\\mathrm J$: apply the closed-form representation to\n$q_{A,V}/E_0$ and multiply the representing operator by $E_0$.\n\nFor an optional spin-half degree of freedom in three dimensions, define\n$$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad S_j=\\frac{\\hbar}{2}\\sigma_j.$$\nFor a core spinor $u=(u_1,u_2)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^2)$,\ndefine the spin-independent form\n$$q^{(2)}_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}[u_1]+q_{A,V}[u_2].$$\nFor a declared dimensionless real coupling $g$, adopt the effective Pauli core\nform\n$$q^{\\mathrm P}_{A,V,g}[u]=q^{(2)}_{A,V}[u]-\\frac{gq}{2m}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(x)^*(S\\cdot B(x))u(x)\\,dx,$$\nwhere $u^*$ is conjugate transpose and $(S\\cdot B)(x)=\\sum_jS_jB_j(x)$.\nInclude this term only in model instances where the resulting form is\nclosable and bounded below, and use its closed form closure and representing\nHamiltonian. No universal semiboundedness for arbitrary $A,V,g$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-magnetic-form-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and let $d\\ge1$ be an integer. Work in\n$H=L^2(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}u\\overline v$, linear in the first\nvariable, using the quotient and complex-function conventions of the linked\nEuclidean $L^2$ definitions. Fix real scalars $m>0$, $\\hbar>0$, and $q$.\nLet $A=(A_1,\\ldots,A_d)\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R^d)$ be real and\nlet $V\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy\n$V\\ge-v_0$ almost everywhere for some finite $v_0\\ge0$. Put\n$\\mathscr D=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $u\\in\\mathscr D$,\ndefine\n$$\\Pi_j^A u=-i\\hbar\\,\\partial_j u-qA_j u\\qquad (1\\le j\\le d).$$\nFor $u,v\\in\\mathscr D$ define the sesquilinear form\n$$q_{A,V}(u,v)=\\frac1{2m}\\sum_{j=1}^d\\langle\\Pi_j^A u,\\Pi_j^A v\\rangle+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}V(x)u(x)\\overline{v(x)}\\,dx,$$\nand write $q_{A,V}[u]=q_{A,V}(u,u)$. Each integral is finite: the test\nfunctions and their magnetic derivatives are bounded with compact support,\nand $V$ is integrable on compact sets. The form is Hermitian, densely\ndefined, and bounded below by $-v_0\\|u\\|_2^2$.\n\nFor $\\varepsilon_0>0$ define its shifted form norm by\n$$\\|u\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}^2=q_{A,V}[u]+(v_0+\\varepsilon_0)\\|u\\|_2^2.$$\nThe form is **closable** when every sequence $(u_n)\\subset\\mathscr D$ with\n$\\|u_n\\|_2\\to0$ and\n$\\|u_n-u_m\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$ as $n,m\\to\\infty$ also satisfies\n$\\|u_n\\|_{q,v_0,\\varepsilon_0}\\to0$. This condition is independent of the\nchoice of $\\varepsilon_0>0$. When it holds, the **closed form closure** is the\ncompletion of $\\mathscr D$ in this norm, identified with its $L^2$ limits;\nthe corresponding closed form is denoted $\\overline q_{A,V}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $F=(F_0,\\ldots,F_{n-1}):U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$ in the componentwise Euclidean sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. Then the **divergence** of $F$ is $\\operatorname{div}F:=\\sum_{i<n}\\partial_iF_i$, the function $U\\to\\mathbb R$ whose value at $p$ is $\\sum_{i<n}\\partial_iF_i(p)$. The partial derivatives are those of [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and the sum is the finite sum used throughout [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Since each $\\partial_iF_i$ is continuous on $U$, so is $\\operatorname{div}F$.\n\nNow let $n=3$ and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be $C^1$ on an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$. Following [[def-cross-product-in-r3]], write the three coordinates of a point and of a vector as $x,y,z$ rather than $0,1,2$, so that $F=(F_x,F_y,F_z)$ means $F=(F_0,F_1,F_2)$ and $\\partial_x,\\partial_y,\\partial_z$ are $\\partial_0,\\partial_1,\\partial_2$. With that naming, the **curl** of $F$ is $\\operatorname{curl}F:=(\\partial_yF_z-\\partial_zF_y,\\ \\partial_zF_x-\\partial_xF_z,\\ \\partial_xF_y-\\partial_yF_x)$, a map $U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ each of whose coordinates is continuous on $U$. In this naming the divergence reads $\\operatorname{div}F=\\partial_xF_x+\\partial_yF_y+\\partial_zF_z$.\n\nBoth operators are defined pointwise from the first partial derivatives of the components, so no differentiability of $F$ beyond $C^1$ is used and no orientation or metric structure enters beyond the standard coordinates of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]. For a $C^1$ scalar function $f$ on $U$, the gradient $\\nabla f=(\\partial_0f,\\ldots,\\partial_{n-1}f)$ is that of [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]]; in the three-coordinate naming, $\\nabla f=(\\partial_xf,\\partial_yf,\\partial_zf)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n(Limit point, isolated point, adherent point, derived set, and dense subset of $\\mathbb{R}$), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n(The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of If $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = L \\ne 0$ then $|f| > |L|/2$ on a punctured neighbourhood of $c$; in particular if $L > 0$ then $f > L/2 > 0$ there applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\nCarathéodory's characterisation: $f$ is differentiable at $c$ if and only if there is $\\varphi : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$, continuous at $c$, with $f(x) - f(c) = \\varphi(x)(x - c)$ for every $x \\in A$, and then $\\varphi$ is unique and $\\varphi(c) = f'(c)$.** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of Sums, scalar multiples, products, absolute values, maxima, minima and quotients with nonvanishing denominator of continuous functions are continuous, as are constants, the identity and every polynomial function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Direct multiplication of the matrices in [A1] gives\n$\\sigma_j^2=I_2$ for each $j$,\n$\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3$,\n$\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\sigma_1$,\n$\\sigma_3\\sigma_1=i\\sigma_2$, and the reversed products have the opposite\nsign. Hence\n$$\\sigma_i\\sigma_j=\\delta_{ij}I_2+i\\sum_{k=1}^3\\epsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k.$$\nFor a core spinor $u$, the product rule [F2] and the definition of $\\Pi_j$\ngive\n$$[\\Pi_i,\\Pi_j]u=iq\\hbar(\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i)u=iq\\hbar\\sum_{k=1}^3\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k u.$$\nAll terms remain compactly supported smooth spinors.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since the Pauli matrices act only on the spin factor, they commute with\neach $\\Pi_j$. Expanding the square using step 1.1 gives\n$$\\left(\\sum_i\\sigma_i\\Pi_i\\right)^2=\\sum_i\\Pi_i^2 I_2+i\\sum_{i,j,k}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k\\Pi_i\\Pi_j=\\sum_i\\Pi_i^2 I_2+\\frac{i}{2}\\sum_{i,j,k}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\sigma_k[\\Pi_i,\\Pi_j].$$\nSubstitute the commutator from step 1.1. The finite identity\n$\\sum_{i,j}\\epsilon_{ijk}\\epsilon_{ij\\ell}=2\\delta_{k\\ell}$ follows by\nlisting the two nonzero ordered pairs when $k=\\ell$ and none when\n$k\\ne\\ell$; the resulting term is $-q\\hbar\\sum_k\\sigma_kB_k$. This proves\nthe displayed Pauli square.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Dividing by $2m$ and using $S_j=\\hbar\\sigma_j/2$ from [A1] rewrites the\nspin term as\n$-q\\hbar\\sigma\\cdot B/(2m)=-qS\\cdot B/m=-2qS\\cdot B/(2m)$.\nComparison with the adopted coupling $-gqS\\cdot B/(2m)$ identifies its\nformal coefficient as $g=2$. The conclusion is only the stated identity of\ncore differential expressions; the postulate separately requires a\nclosable semibounded form before assigning a self-adjoint Pauli Hamiltonian.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The spinor core is nonempty and there is no empty-set parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
