        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-landau-3d-form-diagonalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, $m,\\hbar>0$, and real $q,B$ with $qB\\ne0$. Let\n$c=\\hbar^2/(2m)$, $\\omega_c=|qB|/m$, and\n$E_n=\\hbar\\omega_c(n+\\tfrac12)$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N_0$. On\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^3_{x,y,z};\\mathbb C)$ close the nonnegative form\n$$q^{(3)}_B[u]=\\frac1{2m}\\left(\\|-i\\hbar\\partial_xu\\|_2^2+\\|(-i\\hbar\\partial_y-qBx)u\\|_2^2+\\|-i\\hbar\\partial_zu\\|_2^2\\right),\\qquad u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C).$$\nUse the wave-number Fourier convention\n$$\\mathcal F_yg(k_y)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int e^{-ik_yy}g(y)\\,dy,\\qquad \\mathcal F_zg(k_z)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\int e^{-ik_zz}g(z)\\,dz.$$\nand set\n$$U_3u(\\xi,k_y,k_z)=\\mathcal F_z\\!\\left[\\mathcal F_yu\\!\\left(\\xi+\\frac{\\hbar k_y}{qB},k_y,z\\right)\\right](k_z).$$\nThen $U_3$ extends to a unitary map onto\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^2_{k_y,k_z};\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0))$ by expansion in the\ncomplete transverse oscillator basis $\\psi_n(\\xi)$. If\n$$U_3u(\\xi,k_y,k_z)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty f_n(k_y,k_z)\\psi_n(\\xi),$$\nthe form closure domain and value are exactly\n$$D(\\overline q^{(3)}_B)=\\left\\{u:\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(E_n+ck_z^2\\right)|f_n(k_y,k_z)|^2\\,dk_y\\,dk_z<\\infty\\right\\},$$\n$$\\overline q^{(3)}_B[u]=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\left(E_n+ck_z^2\\right)|f_n(k_y,k_z)|^2\\,dk_y\\,dk_z.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-closed-form-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space, let $Q\\subseteq H$ be a dense linear subspace, and let\n$q:Q\\times Q\\to\\mathbb C$ be a symmetric sesquilinear form, linear in its\nfirst variable. Write $q[u]=q(u,u)$. Suppose that for some $c\\ge0$,\n$q[u]\\ge-c\\|u\\|^2$ for every $u\\in Q$, and that $Q$ is complete in the form\nnorm $\\|u\\|_{q,c}^2=q[u]+(c+1)\\|u\\|^2$. Then there is a unique self-adjoint\noperator $H_q\\ge-cI$ such that\n$$D((H_q+cI)^{1/2})=Q,\\qquad q(u,v)=\\langle H_qu,v\\rangle\\quad(u\\in D(H_q),\\ v\\in Q).$$\nEquivalently, $D(H_q)$ consists exactly of those $u\\in Q$ for which there is\n$f\\in H$ satisfying $q(u,v)=\\langle f,v\\rangle$ for all $v\\in Q$, and then\n$H_qu=f$. Under the $e^{tA}$ generator convention for associated forms, the\nassociated operator is $A=-H_q$. If $q_0$ is a densely defined closed symmetric\nform with $q_0[u]\\ge0$ and $v$ is a symmetric sesquilinear form on $Q(q_0)$\nsatisfying\n$$|v[u,u]|\\le a q_0[u]+b\\|u\\|^2\\quad(u\\in Q(q_0)),\\qquad 0\\le a<1,\\ b\\ge0,$$\nthen $q_0+v$ is closed and bounded below by $-b$ on the same domain. It has a\nunique self-adjoint representing operator there. The form bound is not an\noperator-relative bound.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $T$ be a self-adjoint operator on the nonzero\ncomplex Hilbert space $H$. Then there is a unique regular projection valued\nmeasure $E$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$ such that\n$$\nD(T)=\\Bigl\\{x\\in H:\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda^2\\,dE_x(\\lambda)<\\infty\\Bigr\\},\\qquad Tx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,dE(\\lambda)x\\quad(x\\in D(T)).\n$$\nConversely, if $E$ is a regular projection valued measure on $\\mathbb R$, then\nthe operator $\\int\\lambda\\,dE$ with that domain is self-adjoint and its\nspectral projection valued measure is $E$ again.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. If $f : V \\to \\mathbb C$ belongs to $L^1(\\lambda_n)$,\nthen\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $c=\\hbar^2/(2m)$ and $M_n(k_y,k_z)=E_n+ck_z^2$. Apply [F1] to the\nthree-dimensional core form and then [F2] under the choice hypotheses in [A2]. For\na form-domain vector $F$, the representation criterion says that $F$ is in\nthe operator domain exactly when there exists $G\\in L^2$ with\n$$\\sum_n\\int M_nF_n\\overline{H_n}=\\sum_n\\int G_n\\overline{H_n}$$\nfor every form-domain $H$. If such $G$ exists, fix $n$ and any bounded box\n$K\\subset\\mathbb R^2$. Since $M_n$ is bounded on $K$, both\n$1_KM_nF_n$ and $1_KG_n$ are in $L^2(K)$. Choose the test vector with sole\ncomponent $H_n=1_K(M_nF_n-G_n)$; it belongs to the form domain because its\nindex and support are bounded. The identity forces\n$$\\int_K|M_nF_n-G_n|^2=0.$$\nExhausting $\\mathbb R^2$ by bounded boxes and all $n$ gives $G_n=M_nF_n$\nalmost everywhere, so $MF\\in L^2$. Conversely, if $MF\\in L^2$, then for\nevery form-domain $H$, Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n$$\\sum_n\\int M_nF_n\\overline{H_n}=\\langle MF,H\\rangle,$$\nso [F2] represents the operator by multiplication by $M_n$, with domain\n$\\sum_n\\int M_n^2|F_n|^2<\\infty$. This identification follows under [A2].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The essential range of the multiplier $M_n$ is\n$[E_0,\\infty)$: every $M_n\\ge E_n\\ge E_0$, while the $n=0$ branch\nattains every value in that interval as $k_z$ varies. To verify the spectrum\ndirectly, if $\\lambda\\notin[E_0,\\infty)$ then\n$\\inf_{t\\ge E_0}|t-\\lambda|>0$ and\n$\\sup_{t\\ge E_0}t/|t-\\lambda|<\\infty$. Thus multiplication by\n$(M-\\lambda)^{-1}$ is bounded and maps into the operator domain, so\n$\\lambda$ is in the resolvent. If $\\lambda\\ge E_0$, set\n$k_0=\\sqrt{(\\lambda-E_0)/c}$. For every $\\delta>0$, a sufficiently small\ninterval around $k_0$ (or an interval $0<k_z<\\sqrt{\\delta/c}$ when\n$\\lambda=E_0$), together with any bounded positive-measure interval of\n$k_y$, gives a set of positive finite measure on which\n$|E_0+ck_z^2-\\lambda|<\\delta$. Normalized indicator functions of these\nsets are in the operator domain and have residual norm at most $\\delta$;\nhence $\\lambda$ is in the spectrum. The spectrum is exactly the stated\nhalf-line.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $F=(f_n)$ be any coefficient vector. By [F4], each band measure is\nthe pushforward of $w_n(k_z)\\,dk_z$ under $k_z\\mapsto E_n+ck_z^2$. Splitting\nthe integral into $k_z>0$ and $k_z<0$ and applying [F5] gives, for\n$E>E_n$, the density\n$$\\rho_n(E)=\\frac{w_n\\!\\left(\\sqrt{(E-E_n)/c}\\right)+w_n\\!\\left(-\\sqrt{(E-E_n)/c}\\right)}{2\\sqrt{c(E-E_n)}}.$$\nThe threshold $E_n$ has no atom because its preimage is the null set\n$\\{0\\}$. Therefore each band measure is absolutely continuous, and the\ncountable nonnegative sum of these finite measures remains absolutely\ncontinuous. By [F3] these are all scalar spectral measures of the\nrepresented Hamiltonian. There are no eigenvalues or singular-continuous\nspectral measures. The conclusion is conditional on the adopted model assumptions.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
